{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"fadable-sewable-bovine","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Concept\nAdd a first-class research ticket type for durable, lifecycle-managed investigation work that does not modify the codebase and should not pay software-delivery gate costs. It fills the gap between ephemeral/session-oriented session_log records and implementation-bearing task, story, bug, and epic tickets.\n\n## Proposed semantics\n- research tickets use the ordinary open -> in_progress -> closed lifecycle.\n- research tickets cannot be created in or transitioned to idea status.\n- plan review is inapplicable and cannot be invoked for research tickets.\n- completion verification is inapplicable and cannot be invoked for research tickets.\n- file impact and verification-command metadata cannot be set on research tickets.\n- research tickets cannot have a parent and cannot be made a child through parent-setting or child hierarchy links.\n- research tickets may use non-hierarchical links to other tickets so evidence, discoveries, and resulting implementation work remain connected.\n- research tickets appear in list/search/show operations and normal direct-id lookups.\n- research tickets are excluded from ready and next-batch because those commands enumerate executable implementation work.\n- research tickets retain comments, assignee/claim state, tags, priority, authorship, bridge-safe identity, and the normal audit history needed for collaborative investigation.\n\n## Safety boundary\nThis type is not a gate bypass for code-changing work. The product should define and enforce that a research ticket cannot declare file impact, carry implementation completion evidence, or serve as the claimed work item for a code commit. Research findings that authorize repository changes must create or promote separate ordinary gated tickets and link them to the research ticket.\n\n## Design work before promotion\n- Define the canonical event/schema representation, public Python type, CLI/MCP surface, reducer validation, transition matrix, and compatibility behavior for older readers.\n- Decide which existing non-hierarchical relations are allowed and reject every hierarchy-forming path consistently, including inverse link forms.\n- Define whether claim is allowed directly from open and how default assignee/session ownership works.\n- Define search/list filters and bridge/Jira representation without letting reconciled Jira hierarchy imply a local parent.\n- Add cross-surface behavioral tests proving plan-review, completion-verifier, file-impact, parent, ready, and next-batch prohibitions and proving normal lifecycle/search/link behavior.\n- Document when to use research versus session_log versus task/story/bug/epic.\n\n## Intended outcome\nInvestigations such as architecture research, historical CI-log analysis, OSS prior-art studies, and empirical probes get a durable collaborative work record with an honest lifecycle, while implementation tickets retain the full plan-review, file-impact, and completion-verification discipline.","id":"4bfe-2829-1c56-48d1","parent_id":"","priority":2,"status":"idea","tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Add a lightweight research ticket type"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787017859924538001,"uuid":"974cc916-bf31-42dd-8030-0630748935c9"}